00:00:03.707 --> 00:00:05.498
Let's talk about the sort
of perception of time.

00:00:05.498 --> 00:00:06.746
And what the research shows us,

00:00:06.746 --> 00:00:10.260
is that perception of experience time
is largely different by attention.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.960
Time bothers us when we
pay attention to it.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:17.420
And so the name of the game is
to give people distractions.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.049
It makes time feel like
it went by quickly.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.040
So, give your customers
something to do while waiting.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:28.480
Compare an elevator ride, some companies
use music but usually they use irritating

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:34.780
music, but what if you play them the news
or some kind of interesting podcasts,

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:40.500
and then it almost becomes something,
it's distracting and fun to be waiting.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.950
The other thing to do to manage time
is give people a sense of progress.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:48.200
Okay the one thing we really
don't like is not knowing, right?

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.485
Not knowing when this
thing is going to end.

00:00:51.485 --> 00:00:53.340
Actually, airlines used to be bad at this.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.320
You had a boarding time but it was never
kind of clear exactly when it was.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.790
And now they've shifted to telling you
exactly when the boarding time is, and

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:01.440
they even have a countdown telling you,

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.640
boarding is going to commence
in X amount of minutes.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.960
The exact same amount of time where
you know kind of you have a sense of

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:12.220
progress versus you don't have a sense
of progress, feels completely different.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:18.269
And so, Disney recognizes that it has
people waiting in lines for a ton of time.

00:01:18.269 --> 00:01:23.035
And so what they do is they have these
kind of milepost where they say,

00:01:23.035 --> 00:01:28.724
this is how much time you have left to go,
to give you a sense of what's happening.

00:01:28.724 --> 00:01:29.969
Because we really don't like,

00:01:29.969 --> 00:01:32.700
because we don't like to have
an experience where we don't have control.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:36.100
So you want to give people
a sense of control.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:41.550
What matters is not actual control, but
it's your sense of perceived control.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.800
Do I feel like I'm controlling the time,
that's passing?

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:50.710
Or is this something else controlling
it and I have no control over.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:55.870
Most of the time, customers
are engaging in these experiences and

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.990
they're not cynical.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:01.450
When I go to Disney,
I know they're messing with my mind

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:04.350
with all the information about
the waiting time, but it still works.

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:09.370
You can have an objective time in
which something happened that's very

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:14.060
different than the subjective time,
in which something happened.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:19.910
And so the question is, how do you manage
that subjective sense of elapsed time?

